## Before writing anything

Read what already exists, in this order, and confirm rather than re-ask:

1. `docs/journey/journey-state.md` — aspiration, terrain, position
2. `docs/discovery/` — path maps and actor profiles
3. `docs/stressor-analysis/` — the matrix and the residuals
4. `docs/adr/` — decisions already made and their reasoning
5. The code, if it exists

Most of what a document needs is already recorded. Re-asking questions the
journey answered is how architects stop trusting the tooling, and it produces
documents that contradict the analysis.

Then ask only what is genuinely missing — one question at a time.

## `/restack-solution-doc hld`

**Read** `skills/restack-solution-doc/sections/hld-structure.md` and write from
it.

Two sections carry the value and are the ones most often omitted:

- **Residuals and what they defend against** — each residual, the stressors it
  clears, the ADR behind it, and what new paths it created.
- **What this design does not do** — the explicit non-goals, which prevent the
  standard misreading that everything unmentioned is either included or
  forgotten.

Write to `docs/architecture/HLD.md`. Close with a reflection prompt about what
was hardest to articulate — that is reliably where the design is weakest.

## `/restack-solution-doc lld [component]`

**Read** `skills/restack-solution-doc/sections/lld-structure.md`.

Write one per component that needs it, not per component that exists. The
section names the four cases that justify an LLD; a document written for
completeness will drift, and a drifted LLD is worse than none because people
trust it.

Failure behaviour (section 5) is the part that most repays being written before
the code rather than after.

Write to `docs/architecture/LLD-<component>.md`.

## `/restack-solution-doc deployment`

**Read** the deployment half of
`skills/restack-solution-doc/sections/operational-docs.md`.

Verification steps must be specific enough that two people would agree on
whether the deploy worked, and the rollback section must name what **cannot**
be rolled back. Write to `docs/deployment/DEPLOYMENT.md`.

## `/restack-solution-doc runbook`

**Read** the runbook half of
`skills/restack-solution-doc/sections/operational-docs.md`.

Populate the failure-modes section from the stressor matrix rather than
inventing it: for each significant stressor, what an operator observes, which
residual should absorb it, and what to do when the residual is not working.

Include what **not** to do. It is the highest-value content in any runbook and
is almost never written down. Write to `docs/operations/RUNBOOK.md`.

## `/restack-solution-doc complete`

HLD → LLDs (only where warranted) → deployment → runbook, in that order, each
building on the last.

Carry contradictions forward rather than quietly resolving them: if the runbook
needs an operational lever the LLD does not describe, that is a design gap the
documentation just found, and it is worth more than the documents.

## `/restack-solution-doc update <type>`

Read the current document, establish what changed and why, then update. Where
the change reflects a decision, make sure an ADR exists — documentation is not
a substitute for a decision record.

Never quietly rewrite a design document to match what was built. If the
implementation diverged, that divergence is the finding: either the design was
wrong or the build was, and one of them needs a decision.

## `/restack-solution-doc review <type>`

Assess an existing document against the relevant structure section.

The useful test is not completeness but **usability**: what would a new team
member still have to ask after reading this? Read it as that person, list the
questions, and treat each unanswered one as a gap.

Check specifically for the three failures: residuals documented without their
purpose, happy paths documented without error paths, and confident statements
about existing-system behaviour that were never verified.

**For consistency against the rest of the documentation** — whether this
document still agrees with the ADRs, whether it carries the actors a later
iteration added — use `/restack-design-review consistency`, which owns that
check across the whole document set. Reviewing one document against itself
cannot find drift; drift is a property of the set.

